(333, 'QUST-2-CBE-2025-507', 2390, 'Climate Risk and Digital Innovation: Assessing the Impact of ICT on Food Security in Vulnerable Economies', 'Accepted', NULL, NULL, '0.00', 203, 52, '2026-04-04 13:35:02', '2026-04-04 13:35:02', b'0', '10000', 'Awarded', '* Please be aware that Software/items/books provided by QU are non-allowable items in budget\nQUID Not provided in the document \n* Please add Plagiarism report with percentage not exceeding 10%\n- please attach the student ID and similarity report within the proposal and upload them all at once\n- project ID is missing', 'Awarded', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0),
(334, 'QUST-2-CBE-2025-512', 2391, 'Dynamic Connectedness between Financial Technology (FinTech) and GCC Banking Sectors', 'Accepted', NULL, NULL, '0.00', 204, 52, '2026-04-04 13:35:03', '2026-04-29 07:34:15', b'0', '20000', 'Awarded', '* QUID Not provided in the document \n* Please add Plagiarism report with percentage not exceeding 10%\n- project ID is missing', 'Awarded', 0, NULL, 'Fahad Shafiq and Nedal Alfayoumi, Dynamic Connectedness between Financial Technology (FinTech) and GCC Banking Sectors, 8th International Conference on Entrepreneurship and Sustainability for Impact (ESI): Frontier Technologies for Resilient Economies, Qatar ,2025, Doha, November.', 'Following the initial proposal submitted for the application of ‘Graduate Student Research Grant’, several new additions have been made towards achieving an outcome for the study. The research has seen significant progress and has further evolved into a master’s thesis, currently in its final stage of completion. Moreover, throughout this period, the study has also been presented at several international conferences, receiving an array of positive feedback from academic and industry professionals. \r\nSubstantial additions and improvements have been made to the literature review, data collection, and empirical analysis. The literature review has been expanded to address the gaps from both theoretical and empirical standpoint. A comprehensive dataset has been complied for the targeted GCC banking and FinTech indices using the Bloomberg terminal, covering three major crisis periods namely: the COVID-19 pandemic, the Russia-Ukraine war, and the Gaza Strip conflict. Furthermore, previously the research builds on the Extended Joint Connectedness (EJC) model to account for interconnectedness and systemic spillover between markets during periods of market stress. We took a step further to account for micro-level analysis by incorporating the AR(1) DCC GARCH model for bivariate portfolios to capture portfolio-level risk dynamics and hedging effectiveness. This combined framework not only provides insights for policymakers to make calculated judgments and formulate policies to shield against forecasted economic crises but also allows portfolio managers and financial institutions to hedge against their holdings during periods of heightened uncertainty, allowing various economic segments to utilize this research. The outcome from the empirical findings have also been validated using robustness checks to ensure the stability of the findings.\r\nWith increasing FinTech adoption within the GCC banking systems, while furthering their national strategic vision goals, our study addresses an important dimension of innovation related risk. By examining and understanding risk propagation between FinTech and GCC banking markets during crisis periods when connectedness intensifies, allowing for increased contagion within the system, this research allows for informed designs and risk management strategies.\r\nThe recent US-Iran war, classified as a period of high uncertainty and more so, stagnation of oil markets, is a key threat to the GCC banking markets due to their dependence on energy revenues. Volatile markets such as FinTech may have tested flight of investors to safe havens such as Gold and other precious metals. Understanding asymmetry of these relationships and tackling avenues to hedge against these uncertain periods further justify our motivation for this research.\r\nFindings indicate that FinTech acts as a net transmitter of shocks during periods of uncertainty, while larger GCC banking markets such as Saudi Arabia play a key role of structural transmitter. In contrast, smaller markets such as Oman and Bahrain remain as consistent net receivers of spillovers, potentially due to their targeted domestic policies and lower rate of FinTech adoption, shielding them through limited contagion. In addition, market roles vary across crises, making them crises dependent. Consistent patterns are observed particularly during geopolitical episodes (Russia-Ukraine war and Gaza Strip conflict) compared to broader global pandemic (COVID-19). \r\nThe research has initially been presented at the 8th International Conference on Entrepreneurship and Sustainability for Impact (ESI): Frontier Technologies for Resilient Economies 2025 and has attracted researchers and industry academics due to its relevance to the GCC economy and latest methodological contribution used to analyze systemic spillovers and portfolio hedging effectiveness. In addition, the research proposal has also been accepted for the second time at the Risk, Banking, Finance Society (RBF 2026) Global Finance Forum in Saudi Arabia. Most recently, the study has been nominated for presentation at Qatar University’s Annual Research Forum & Exhibition 2026.\r\nIn addition to the research outputs, academic engagement, and conference participation, this research has contributed to improvement of the student’s development in advanced econometric modelling skills and strong understanding of complex risk management analysis in finance.', 'save', 0),
